%I A399623 #30 Sep 14 2026 19:06:19
%S A399623 1,1,1,2,0,2,3,2,7,3,4,5,3,6,4,7,5,9,4,6,8,5,7,10,13,6,9,11,8,12,14,
%T A399623 15,16,10,17,9,8,13,11,18,19,12,20,10,14,21,15,22,16,11,13,17,28,12,
%U A399623 23,24,25,18,14,19,26,15,20,27,16,29,21,30,17,22,31
%N A399623 For each positive integer value v, place three copies of v at the earliest available positions k, k+v, k+2v; any unoccupied positions are set to 0.
%C A399623 a(5) = 0 because it seems no number can ever be placed there.
%C A399623 It seems that every position gets filled except for n = 5, which holds true up through n = 3.5*10^10 and v = 1.2*10^9.
%C A399623 Suppose you stop the process at a large value of v and look at the earliest unfilled index n > 5. The ratio n/v seems to converge to a constant 2.214319... = A305326.
%C A399623 In order for n = 5 to ever receive a number, this ratio would have to creep down to almost 1, contradicting its strongly apparent convergence.
%C A399623 Equivalent to A164888, in which the k-th term gives the index of the first occurrence of k in the present sequence. - _N. J. A. Sloane_, Sep 14 2026
%H A399623 Eric Fox, <a href="/A399623/b399623.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A399623 For v = 1, set a(1) = a(2) = a(3) = 1.
%e A399623 For v = 2, set a(4) = a(6) = a(8) = 2.
%e A399623 For v = 3, a(5) is available but a(5+3=8) is not, so skip forward, set a(7) = a(10) = a(13) = 3.
%e A399623 For v = 4, a(5) and a(5+4=9) are available but a(5+2*4=13) is not, so skip forward, a(9) is available but a(9+4=13) is not, so skip forward, set a(11) = a(15) = a(19) = 4.
%Y A399623 Cf. A026242, A164888, A305326, A307664.
%K A399623 nonn,new
%O A399623 1,4
%A A399623 _Eric Fox_, Sep 05 2026
